1. Test Connectivity
ansible all -m ping
2. Check Hostname
ansible all -a "hostname"
3. Check Uptime
ansible all -a "uptime"
4. Check Logged-in Users
ansible all -a "who"
5. Check Current User
ansible all -a "whoami"
6. Check Disk Space
ansible all -a "df -h"
7. Check Memory Usage
ansible all -a "free -m"
8. Check CPU Information
ansible all -a "lscpu"
9. Check OS Version
ansible all -a "cat /etc/os-release"
10. Check Kernel Version
ansible all -a "uname -r"
11. Check IP Address
ansible all -a "ip addr"
12. Gather System Facts
ansible all -m setup

Only network facts:

ansible all -m setup -a "filter=ansible_default_ipv4"
13. Install Git

Ubuntu:

ansible all -b -m apt -a "name=git state=present update_cache=yes"

RHEL:

ansible all -b -m dnf -a "name=git state=present"
14. Update All Packages (Ubuntu)
ansible all -b -m apt -a "upgrade=dist update_cache=yes"
15. Create a User
ansible all -b -m user -a "name=devops state=present"
16. Delete a User
ansible all -b -m user -a "name=devops state=absent remove=yes"
17. Create a Directory
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/demo state=directory mode=0755"
18. Delete a Directory
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/demo state=absent"
19. Copy a File
ansible all -m copy -a "src=index.html dest=/tmp/index.html"
20. Download a File
ansible all -m get_url -a "url=https://example.com/file.txt dest=/tmp/file.txt"
21. Install Nginx
ansible web -b -m apt -a "name=nginx state=present"
22. Start Nginx
ansible web -b -m service -a "name=nginx state=started enabled=yes"
23. Restart Nginx
ansible web -b -m service -a "name=nginx state=restarted"
24. Stop Nginx
ansible web -b -m service -a "name=nginx state=stopped"
25. Reboot Servers
ansible all -b -m reboot
26. Shut Down Servers
ansible all -b -m shell -a "shutdown -h now"
27. Check Running Processes
ansible all -a "ps -ef"
28. Kill a Process
ansible all -b -m shell -a "pkill nginx"
29. Check Open Ports
ansible all -a "ss -tulnp"
30. Check Systemd Services
ansible all -a "systemctl list-units --type=service"
31. Check Docker Containers
ansible docker -a "docker ps"
32. Check Kubernetes Nodes
ansible k8s-master -a "kubectl get nodes"
33. Find Large Files
ansible all -a "find /var -type f -size +100M"
34. Check Failed Services
ansible all -a "systemctl --failed"
35. View Recent Logs
ansible all -a "journalctl -n 50"
36. Check SSH Service
ansible all -b -m service -a "name=ssh state=started"
37. Add an SSH Public Key
ansible all -b -m authorized_key \
-a "user=ubuntu key='ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... user@example'"
38. Check Time Synchronization
ansible all -a "timedatectl"
39. Run Different Commands Only on the Web Group
ansible web -a "hostname"
40. Limit Execution to One Host
ansible all -l web1 -a "uptime"
41. Execute Commands in Parallel
ansible all -f 20 -a "hostname"

-f 20 tells Ansible to manage up to 20 hosts concurrently.

42. Dry Run (Supported Modules)
ansible all -m copy -a "src=test.txt dest=/tmp/test.txt" --check
43. Use Verbose Output
ansible all -m ping -vvv
Commonly Used Modules
Module	Purpose
ping	Test connectivity
setup	Gather system facts
command	Run a command (default if -m is omitted)
shell	Run shell commands with pipes, redirects, etc.
copy	Copy files to remote hosts
fetch	Copy files from remote hosts
file	Create/remove files and directories
user	Manage users
group	Manage groups
service	Start, stop, restart, and enable services
apt	Manage packages on Ubuntu/Debian
dnf	Manage packages on RHEL 8+/Fedora
yum	Manage packages on older RHEL/CentOS
reboot	Reboot remote systems
authorized_key	Manage SSH public keys
Example: Daily Health Check

This sequence gives a quick overview of server health:

ansible all -a "hostname"
ansible all -a "uptime"
ansible all -a "df -h"
ansible all -a "free -m"
ansible all -a "systemctl --failed"
ansible all -a "journalctl -p err -n 20"

These are the types of ad hoc commands commonly used for day-to-day Linux administration and operational checks before moving to reusable playbooks for larger automation tasks.
